Dr. Elena Vasquez pulled the raw spectral logs into a narrower band, filtering out the familiar hiss of water vapor and the low-frequency rumble that always crept in when the wind struck the dome at just the wrong angle. The anomaly remained. It was not loud enough to be obvious, nor regular enough to be mechanical. It pulsed like breath held too long—three quick rises, a pause, then one slow fall.

She copied the sequence into her notebook by hand, as if writing it down might make it more real. Three weeks ago she had dismissed it as calibration drift. Two weeks ago she had checked every instrument in the building twice. Last week she had stopped sleeping properly. Now, with dawn still a pale smear beyond the eastern ridge and coffee gone cold beside her keyboard, she could no longer pretend uncertainty was caution. It was fear dressed as professionalism.

The signal came from a region of sky where nothing should have been transmitting: far beyond Neptune, in the cold dark where sunlight thinned to memory. There were no satellites there, no probes close enough to account for it, no known natural source that produced such structured light. And yet the pattern repeated every 17 hours and 42 minutes with a consistency that made Elena’s chest tighten each time she saw it.

She ran one more cross-check against archived data from other observatories. At first, nothing. Then, buried in an old survey log from Chile, she found it: a faint echo of the same oscillation, recorded six years earlier and flagged as noise. Another appeared in a Japanese dataset from four years ago.

